{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c031",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Weekly Welfare Fund payment cannot be squared with Section 13-H.1 of the 1981-84 Collective Bargaining Agreement, which requires payments \"on the basis of weekly employment\" to be made \"at least once a month, unless the Trustees of the Fund determine that more frequent payments are necessary.\" Whether the letter supersedes that provision, and for whom, is a live question in any delinquency claim.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": "\"Made available\" no later than ten days after the payroll week is not the same as paid or received, so the date on which a contribution becomes delinquent is undefined. The letter is also undated (\"July , 1988\"), leaving the ratification-plus-30-days trigger to be fixed by extrinsic evidence.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c032",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"The Grandfather or Grandmother of a Regular Situation Holder or Group I Extra or of his or her spouse at the time the grandparent dies\" is syntactically ambiguous: the closing phrase may qualify the spousal relationship, the employment status, or merely fix the date of the event. The letter also does not say whether step- or in-law grandparents count, which is the very inconsistency it was written to cure.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c033",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The cut-off is \"a death occurring prior to the date hereof,\" but the letter bears no date at all (\"July , 1988\"), so the retroactivity line has no fixed reference point. Whether the operative date is the letter, ratification, or execution of the Memorandum is unresolved.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c034",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The replacement first sentence of Section 13-K.2 is unreadable in the executed document (\"may elect to accumulate a ll ef hi ... unused\"), so the new accumulation right cannot be ascertained from the words. The accompanying deletion of the fifth sentence also breaks off mid-quotation, leaving it unclear how much is deleted.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c035",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Certain neutrals\" are neither named nor made identifiable, and the letter fixes no consequence or default mechanism if the parties meet within sixty days and fail to agree on designations. What the parties are actually bound to do is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c036",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The obligation is only \"to negotiate the conditions\" for a run, with no obligation to establish it and no standard for the negotiation. \"When the normal daily order reaches 2500 copies\" also leaves \"normal daily order\" undefined — over what period, and whether Sunday counts.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c037",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The trigger for the Monday Lancaster assignment depends on the parties having \"agreed upon satisfactory arrangements\" and on a \"revised Glen Burnie agreement\" being \"put into effect,\" none of which is defined or dated, so the thirty-day clock may never start. The proviso that the delivery \"shall not involve The Times in increased costs\" sets no baseline for measuring cost.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c038",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The duty to negotiate is conditioned on the parties having \"resolved satisfactorily any grievances, differences and disputes\" about Edison work and staffing — an open-ended condition with no objective test or deadline. \"Additional portions\" of the Magazine and the no-increased-cost proviso are likewise unquantified.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c039",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The amendment says the change to \"7.678%\" merely reflects \"the present welfare contribution percentage,\" yet Section 13-H.1 as written states 6-1/2%, and no effective date is given for the new rate. When the 7.678% obligation began, and on what base it is computed, is not stated.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35},
     {"issue": "The 7.678% figure does not match the 7.68% health and welfare contribution rate recited in paragraph 8 of the 1992 Memorandum of Agreement, which also caps total plan contributions at 15.68%. Which percentage governs contributions in the overlapping period must be resolved between the two documents.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c040",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The insertion is a quotation that is never closed — add \"and an after tax deduction from wages of $3.67 after the words \"per shift\" — so it cannot be told whether the inserted words end at \"$3.67\" or include the locating phrase, or which occurrence of \"per shift\" is meant. The amended text of Section 13-H.1 is therefore indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40},
     {"issue": "This subparagraph calls the $3.67 an \"after tax deduction from wages,\" while paragraph 2(a) of the Memorandum says the same $3.67 per shift \"shall be contributed to the Welfare Fund.\" Whether the Fund is owed that amount as an employer contribution cannot be answered from either provision alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c041",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The first-year figure is illegible in the executed document (\"3/31/87 = §.00 per shift\"), so the additional contribution for the initial contract year cannot be determined from the writing. The schedule also does not say whether the five-shift weekly cap in Section 13-H.1 applies to these amounts.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": "\"Casuals\" is a term Section 13-H.1 never uses; that section identifies the class by exclusion — employees who are not regular situation holders, Group 1 or Group 2 extras, or Group 3 extras working more than 180 days. Which employees the increased per-shift contributions cover requires reconciling the two texts.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
